<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; text-align: center; padding: 15px 0; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Learning to Balance</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How to Balance Social and School Activities</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Concept: Explain complex ideas with simple examples. Communication: Assist in clear communication of plans and roles.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can explain time management strategies with simple examples and gestures (e.g., points to a timer to indicate time management). Can assist in communication roles through miming or visuals (e.g., points to someone for "notetaker" and uses "writing" with a pencil gesture to indicate the role). Can use basic bilingual terms to bridge understanding in discussions on school-life balance (e.g., "Take a break and relax, like 'descanso,' for a few minutes to clear your mind.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <!-- STAGE 1 -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher begins by projecting three complex statements about school-life balance (e.g., "Effective allocation of temporal resources necessitates proactive engagement with academic commitments whilst fostering robust social interactions."). Students work in pairs to rephrase one statement into simpler terms. Teacher elicits simplified versions from the class.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to simplify a complex idea and use a gesture or visual to aid understanding. For example, "When I say 'proactive engagement,' I mean 'taking initiative' – like planning your schedule ahead of time (teacher makes a planning gesture or points to a calendar visual). I have been organizing my time more effectively, so I manage my workload. If I had more flexibility, I would prioritize downtime." The teacher emphasizes the short /ɪ/ in 'activities' and the long /iː/ in 'relieve' while giving suggestions: "Taking these activities seriously can help relieve stress."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does it mean to 'mediate' an idea?" (Expected answer: "To make it easier to understand," "to simplify," "to bridge understanding.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why might we use a gesture or a simple example when explaining?" (Expected answer: "To make it clearer," "to help someone who doesn't understand the words," "to remember better.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>

        <!-- STAGE 2 -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Learning to Balance".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio recording (1.5-2 minutes) of two students, Ana and Luis, discussing their approaches to "Learning to Balance" for their "School-Life Balance Plan" project. One student uses slightly more complex language about "time management" and "mindfulness," while the other struggles to fully grasp it. The recording features examples of giving suggestions and expressing opinions on balance. The audio also includes phrases using present perfect continuous and modals for possibility. (e.g., Ana: "I have been exploring various mindfulness techniques to relieve stress." Luis: "Mindfulness? You mean like... 'relajar la mente'?" Ana: "Exactly! Or you might try setting boundaries for your social life.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Explain complex ideas with simple examples; assist in clear communication of plans and roles; use basic bilingual terms to bridge understanding in discussions on school-life balance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What are Ana and Luis trying to achieve in their conversation?" (Expected answer: "To understand each other's school-life balance strategies.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students listen again and mark statements as True or False (e.g., "Ana has been practicing mindfulness for a long time." "Luis understands all of Ana's complex vocabulary.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match complex terms from Ana to Luis's simpler rephrasing or gestures (e.g., "mindfulness" -> "relajar la mente," "allocate time" -> "schedule specific periods").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>

        <!-- STAGE 3 -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice explaining complex ideas simply, using gestures, and employing bilingual terms to bridge understanding. Focus on present perfect continuous for ongoing efforts, second conditional for hypothetical improvements, and modals for possibility (should, might, can, could). Vocabulary: balance, routine, activities, hobbies, schedule, stress, relaxation, mindfulness, time management, prioritize, relieve. Pronunciation of /ɪ/ in "activities" and /iː/ in "relieve." Pragmatic: Giving suggestions, expressing opinions on balance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Simplify & Gesture Match:</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with five complex sentences related to school-life balance (e.g., "Maintaining optimal cognitive function necessitates periodic restorative intervals."). Students are given a bank of simpler phrases and suggested gestures/mimes. They match each complex sentence to its simpler explanation and the appropriate gesture. (e.g., "Restorative intervals" matches "take short breaks" with a "rest" gesture). Students then say the simpler version, practicing the correct pronunciation of /ɪ/ and /iː/ in words like 'activities' and 'relieve.'</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Hypothetical Balance & Mediation Chains:</b> In small groups, students complete sentence starters about their "School-Life Balance Plans" using present perfect continuous or second conditional (e.g., "I have been *struggling with procrastination* this semester, so I *might need to set clearer boundaries*." or "If I had *more effective time management*, I *would prioritize my hobbies*."). One student says their sentence. Another student then acts as a mediator, rephrasing the idea simply, adding a suggestion using a modal verb, and perhaps a bilingual term if needed (e.g., "So, you're saying you've been putting things off, like 'dejar para después'? You should really try to organize your schedule better.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Role Clarification Charades:</b> Students are given cards with "roles" in a group project about school-life balance (e.g., "Notetaker," "Timekeeper," "Presenter," "Idea Generator"). In pairs, one student picks a card and must explain the role to their partner using only gestures, miming, or simple, short English phrases, without saying the role name. The partner guesses the role. Then they switch. This practices assisting in clear communication of roles.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>

        <!-- STAGE 4 -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Learning to Balance".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Group Presentation & Mediation Session.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b> Students are divided into groups of 3-4. Each student has their individual "School-Life Balance Plan" (developed in previous lessons). Within their group, they will take turns presenting a key strategy or aspect from their plan that they "have been working on." The presenter must identify one potentially complex idea in their plan (e.g., "fostering resilience through varied activities," "implementing effective self-regulation") and be prepared to explain it using simple examples, gestures, or a basic bilingual term (e.g., "resilience, like 'fortaleza,' means bouncing back when things are tough"). Other group members act as their "audience," asking clarifying questions. The presenter's role is to mediate understanding, ensuring all group members grasp the core concepts of their plan and offer suggestions for improvement (e.g., "You might try to schedule your relaxation time").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> This task directly engages students in sharing and understanding individual strategies for "How to Balance Social and School Activities" through the lens of "Learning to Balance," requiring them to actively mediate understanding for their peers regarding their "School-Life Balance Plans." They encourage inclusive communication where all group members share their perspectives and contribute strategies for managing stress effectively.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>

        <!-- STAGE 5 -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Feedback Form.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student selects one specific "time management strategy" or "relaxation technique" from their "School-Life Balance Plan." They then explain this to a new partner (or the teacher), focusing on mediating understanding. They must provide a simple example and use at least one gesture or visual cue (e.g., pointing to a clock, miming deep breathing) to clarify the concept. They should also demonstrate giving suggestions and expressing opinions on balance (e.g., "I think this helps me focus"). The partner uses a simple checklist to indicate if the explanation was clear, if gestures were used effectively, and if they understood the strategy. The teacher uses an observation checklist to note the effective use of mediation strategies.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target sounds (/ɪ/ in 'activities', /iː/ in 'relieve').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of target vocabulary related to balance, time management, and relaxation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanation using simple language and examples.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of gestures/visuals or bilingual terms to bridge understanding.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to give suggestions and express opinions regarding the balance plan.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrated use of present perfect continuous, second conditional, and modals for possibility.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one idea from your 'School-Life Balance Plan' that you found challenging to explain simply, and how did you try to mediate it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did using gestures or simple examples help you communicate your plan to others today?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>

        <!-- STAGE 6 -->
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Reflecting on this lesson, how has practicing mediation skills improved your ability to communicate your ideas about school-life balance?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"In what real-life situations outside of school could these mediation skills (explaining simply, using gestures, bridging understanding) be useful?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Today's lesson focused on mediation, allowing students to consolidate their learning on "How to Balance Social and School Activities" by explaining their personal "School-Life Balance Plans." The activities in Stage 3 and 4 effectively engaged students in simplifying complex ideas and using non-verbal cues. The use of bilingual terms (e.g., 'descanso', 'fortaleza') was a natural and effective way to bridge understanding for B1.3 learners. Some students still hesitated to use gestures, relying heavily on verbal explanations. For future mediation tasks, I might introduce more explicit visual aids or a game-like activity focused solely on non-verbal communication to build confidence. The integration of present perfect continuous, second conditional, and modals for possibility felt organic within the context of discussing ongoing efforts and hypothetical improvements in their balance plans.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the theme "How to Balance Social and School Activities." The skills of mediation, particularly explaining complex ideas and assisting communication, are transferable and will be crucial for future collaborative projects and presentations across various themes, preparing students for real-world communication where clarity and understanding are paramount.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>